1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:04,210
A buyer is sitting across the table from
a woman who has run the same business for

2
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:06,310
twenty five years.

3
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:07,670
It makes money.

4
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:10,650
It has made money every
year since she started it.

5
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:14,720
He has already looked at the
numbers and he likes them.

6
00:00:14,770 --> 00:00:17,010
Then he asks her one more question.

7
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:20,590
What happens here if you
are not around for a month.

8
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:22,800
And she starts to answer.

9
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,710
She gets about four words in.

10
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:27,050
And then she stops.

11
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:30,450
Because she is doing the
arithmetic in her head,

12
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:33,870
and the arithmetic is not going her way.

13
00:00:35,080 --> 00:00:39,250
That pause costs her more than
anything else in the room.

14
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:44,750
And there is a business hiding inside
it that almost nobody is running.

15
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:49,550
This is The Operator Economy, where
we show you how to use AI to build,

16
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:52,670
own, and operate a
sustainable business of one.

17
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:56,480
Today we are building a
sale readiness practice.

18
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:01,450
One person, no licence in most of the
country, working with owners years before

19
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:03,050
anybody signs anything.

20
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:06,890
By the end you will know exactly what
this practice can honestly charge for,

21
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:11,250
and the one number that decides
whether it works at all.

22
00:01:11,630 --> 00:01:13,770
Start with how a small business
actually changes hands,

23
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:17,740
because the system is not broken and it is
worth understanding before we criticise

24
00:01:17,790 --> 00:01:18,790
any part of it.

25
00:01:18,950 --> 00:01:20,250
An owner decides it is time.

26
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:21,300
She calls a broker.

27
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:23,710
The broker values the
business, prepares a listing,

28
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:28,220
finds buyers, manages the process, and gets
paid a percentage when the deal closes.

29
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:29,510
That last part matters.

30
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:31,550
The broker is paid on success.

31
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:34,290
And for an owner, that
is genuinely good news.

32
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:36,670
She has no cash to spend on a maybe.

33
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:39,560
She does not want to write cheques
for a sale that might not happen.

34
00:01:39,610 --> 00:01:43,230
Success fees put the risk on the broker,
and for a business that is already in good

35
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:44,930
shape, the whole thing works.

36
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:46,730
Two things have changed
around that system.

37
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:48,010
The first is volume.

38
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:52,790
A very large number of these businesses
are reaching the end of one owner's working

39
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:54,890
life at roughly the same time.

40
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:56,690
There are millions of them.

41
00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:58,190
The second is the buyers.

42
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:01,210
There are more of them now, and
some do this professionally,

43
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:04,530
buying up small operators in the
same trade one after another.

44
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:07,950
Which means a buyer today can afford to be
picky in a way a buyer twenty years ago

45
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,990
could not. Ask it plainly, then.

46
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:13,600
Here is the question this
episode is going to answer.

47
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:18,970
If every single professional standing
around that sale gets paid only when it

48
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:23,130
closes, then who is getting
paid to make it closeable.

49
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:24,780
Nobody is.

50
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:28,990
And that is not an oversight
that somebody is about to fix.

51
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:34,090
It is a hole with a specific shape, produced
by the way everyone in that room earns

52
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:35,140
a living.

53
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:37,350
Which is exactly why it is still open.

54
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:40,790
Let us start with the part
that surprised me most.

55
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:46,180
Listing a business for sale and selling
a business are not the same event.

56
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:48,070
They are not even close.

57
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:52,030
Work from the Exit Planning Institute
puts it at roughly three in ten.

58
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:57,050
That is how many businesses go on the
market and actually find a buyer and close.

59
00:02:57,100 --> 00:02:58,490
Worth saying who that is.

60
00:02:58,540 --> 00:03:02,780
The Exit Planning Institute trains
and certifies exit planners,

61
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:05,210
so they are not a neutral party here.

62
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:08,520
They have an interest in
owners feeling unprepared.

63
00:03:08,570 --> 00:03:11,890
Take the number as directional
rather than precise.

64
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:15,110
Even directionally, it
reframes the whole thing.

65
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:20,270
The default outcome of putting your business
up for sale is that it does not sell.

66
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:23,650
Hold that against what
buyers are actually doing.

67
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:27,630
The trade association for business
brokers runs a survey every quarter where

68
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:31,050
advisers report what actually closed.

69
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:35,930
In one recent quarter, almost nine in ten
deals above five million dollars drew at

70
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:37,510
least three offers.

71
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:40,030
A third of them drew ten or more.

72
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:44,279
That is advisers reporting their own
closings rather than an audited database,

73
00:03:44,329 --> 00:03:45,960
so again, directional.

74
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:51,310
But put those two things side by
side and something odd falls out.

75
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:54,860
Buyers are competing hard
for some businesses.

76
00:03:54,910 --> 00:03:58,870
And most businesses that go
to market never sell at all.

77
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:00,530
Both of those are true at once.

78
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,290
Which means the problem is almost
never that there is nobody out there.

79
00:04:04,660 --> 00:04:07,470
The problem is what happens
when somebody finally looks.

80
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:09,550
Back to her question, then.

81
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:12,650
What happens here if you
are not around for a month.

82
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:14,730
That is not small talk.

83
00:04:15,019 --> 00:04:20,480
That is the buyer checking whether he is
purchasing a business or purchasing a job.

84
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:24,230
Because here is the thing that
took me a while to see properly.

85
00:04:24,650 --> 00:04:26,710
He is not buying her revenue.

86
00:04:27,180 --> 00:04:29,150
Revenue is the evidence.

87
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:34,940
What he is actually buying is a machine
that produces revenue after she stops

88
00:04:34,990 --> 00:04:38,590
touching it. And if that
machine turns out to be her,

89
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:42,650
walking around, remembering things,
then there is nothing to buy.

90
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:44,290
She cannot hand it over.

91
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:46,730
It does not survive the handover.

92
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:49,210
Value only transfers

93
00:04:49,660 --> 00:04:53,900
if the business runs without
the person selling it.

94
00:04:53,950 --> 00:04:58,530
That failure shows up in three shapes,
and they look different from the outside.

95
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:00,730
Sometimes it is the relationships.

96
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:04,010
Every significant customer
is loyal to her personally,

97
00:05:04,060 --> 00:05:07,520
and nobody has any idea whether
they stay when she goes.

98
00:05:07,570 --> 00:05:09,490
Sometimes it is concentration.

99
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:13,210
Two clients are most of the revenue, and
both of them came in through the owner

100
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:16,310
personally. And sometimes
it is just the records.

101
00:05:16,750 --> 00:05:21,130
The business genuinely makes money, and
there is no way for a stranger to confirm it

102
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:22,330
in an afternoon.

103
00:05:22,820 --> 00:05:23,970
Three different problems.

104
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:25,630
Same underlying one.

105
00:05:26,180 --> 00:05:31,230
There is a thing the buyer needs to inspect,
and it is not written down anywhere.

106
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:32,280
It is in her head.

107
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:35,990
And a buyer cannot price
what he cannot inspect.

108
00:05:36,140 --> 00:05:37,610
So he does one of two things.

109
00:05:37,780 --> 00:05:39,690
He discounts it, or he walks.

110
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:42,200
Fine, an owner says at this point.

111
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:44,770
I will sort all that out before I sell.

112
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:47,490
Here is the problem with that plan.

113
00:05:48,020 --> 00:05:53,910
The same research finds that roughly half
of business exits are not planned at all.

114
00:05:54,690 --> 00:05:56,930
They are what they call the five Ds.

115
00:05:57,360 --> 00:05:58,960
Death, disability,

116
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:01,800
divorce, disagreement, distress.

117
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:05,830
Sit with that for a second, because
it changes the shape of the decision.

118
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:10,370
It is not that you should get ready
early because early is tidier.

119
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:12,350
It is that about half the time,

120
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:14,950
the timing is not yours to pick.

121
00:06:15,250 --> 00:06:17,130
You do not get to schedule this.

122
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:19,010
That is the honest version.

123
00:06:19,380 --> 00:06:20,690
Put those together.

124
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:22,320
An owner needs to get ready,

125
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:26,990
cannot reliably choose when,
and mostly has not started.

126
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:30,070
Over half of them intend to
be out within five years.

127
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:33,430
About a quarter have had
an actual valuation done.

128
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:35,770
Fewer than one in ten has an estate plan.

129
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:39,790
And for most of them, the business
is not part of the retirement.

130
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:41,590
It is the retirement.

131
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:43,990
It is the large majority
of everything they own.

132
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:45,700
So who helps.

133
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:48,640
Look at who is actually in that room.

134
00:06:48,690 --> 00:06:50,990
The broker, paid when it closes.

135
00:06:51,040 --> 00:06:53,050
The accountant, busy at the deal.

136
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:55,630
The lawyer, papering the deal.

137
00:06:56,150 --> 00:07:00,190
The wealth adviser, who becomes very
interested the moment there are proceeds to

138
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:01,130
manage,

139
00:07:01,180 --> 00:07:03,070
and proceeds do not exist yet.

140
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:06,960
Every one of them is
compensated by the transaction.

141
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:11,750
And there is a phrase that gets used
about this, usually with some pride.

142
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:13,790
We only get paid when you do.

143
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:18,610
Which is a genuinely good deal, right up
until you notice the other half of it.

144
00:07:18,660 --> 00:07:23,010
It also means not one person in that room
is paid to sit down with her three years

145
00:07:23,060 --> 00:07:26,290
out and tell her the truth
about what she has built.

146
00:07:26,790 --> 00:07:29,370
That work has no owner and no budget line.

147
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:31,170
And that is the vacancy.

148
00:07:31,484 --> 00:07:34,598
Fair objection at this point,
and it is the obvious one.

149
00:07:34,648 --> 00:07:38,507
If this were a real business,
somebody would already be doing it.

150
00:07:38,557 --> 00:07:40,341
And somebody is.

151
00:07:40,391 --> 00:07:42,031
Three somebodies, in fact.

152
00:07:42,243 --> 00:07:46,183
Large companies preparing to be acquired
hire people to assemble a defensible

153
00:07:46,233 --> 00:07:49,223
package before anybody
starts asking questions.

154
00:07:49,623 --> 00:07:50,853
That is a profession.

155
00:07:51,343 --> 00:07:55,953
Software companies going through a security
audit hire specialists to get them ready

156
00:07:56,003 --> 00:07:59,153
first, on a fixed fee, against a standard.

157
00:07:59,363 --> 00:08:01,253
That is a whole industry.

158
00:08:01,303 --> 00:08:06,113
And there are certified exit planners,
who do a version of this for owners,

159
00:08:06,163 --> 00:08:09,333
usually attached to a wealth
practice or a brokerage.

160
00:08:09,683 --> 00:08:11,673
So the job is real.

161
00:08:11,723 --> 00:08:14,593
It is paid work at three separate scales.

162
00:08:14,843 --> 00:08:18,853
It just does not exist for the woman
with the twenty five year old business.

163
00:08:19,083 --> 00:08:21,533
And that is not because
nobody thought of it.

164
00:08:21,663 --> 00:08:26,813
It is because the fee that works at her
size is too small for a firm to staff.

165
00:08:27,103 --> 00:08:31,252
And the pricing model that would fix
that, taking a slice of the sale,

166
00:08:31,382 --> 00:08:34,013
is the one thing you
are not allowed to do.

167
00:08:34,063 --> 00:08:38,153
Which we will come back to, because it is
the single most important constraint in

168
00:08:38,203 --> 00:08:39,803
this entire business.

169
00:08:40,343 --> 00:08:43,493
Picture the company that
belongs in that gap.

170
00:08:43,683 --> 00:08:44,683
Not a broker.

171
00:08:44,753 --> 00:08:46,093
Not an adviser.

172
00:08:46,143 --> 00:08:48,453
A practice that does one job.

173
00:08:48,763 --> 00:08:52,803
It makes a business able to
survive being inspected.

174
00:08:53,303 --> 00:08:54,973
At maturity it looks like this.

175
00:08:55,113 --> 00:08:59,273
A standing relationship with a handful of
accountants and advisers who meet these

176
00:08:59,323 --> 00:09:01,873
owners years before anybody is ready.

177
00:09:02,243 --> 00:09:06,123
Repeat work, because a business
that gets ready once drifts back.

178
00:09:06,503 --> 00:09:11,253
Eventually a lighter, cheaper first product
for owners who are not sure they need

179
00:09:11,303 --> 00:09:12,813
the full thing.

180
00:09:12,863 --> 00:09:14,513
That is the business.

181
00:09:15,023 --> 00:09:17,523
Now let us talk about
whether you can build it.

182
00:09:17,573 --> 00:09:21,153
The instinct here is that
this is an M and A business,

183
00:09:21,203 --> 00:09:23,893
and that you would need deal
experience to touch it.

184
00:09:24,513 --> 00:09:26,213
I do not think that is right.

185
00:09:26,903 --> 00:09:30,293
I think this is an operations
business wearing a suit.

186
00:09:31,213 --> 00:09:35,893
The skill it actually needs is being able
to walk into a company and see where it

187
00:09:35,943 --> 00:09:37,953
depends on one person.

188
00:09:38,003 --> 00:09:40,613
Which is not a finance skill.

189
00:09:40,663 --> 00:09:42,943
It is the thing you learn
by running something,

190
00:09:43,863 --> 00:09:47,733
or by rebuilding something
somebody else ran badly.

191
00:09:48,383 --> 00:09:51,233
I will be straight with
you about my own position.

192
00:09:52,058 --> 00:09:57,548
I have no transaction experience and this
episode is not going to pretend otherwise.

193
00:09:57,598 --> 00:10:02,228
What I have done is be the single
point of failure in businesses I ran,

194
00:10:02,678 --> 00:10:05,168
which is the exact condition
this practice removes.

195
00:10:05,948 --> 00:10:09,148
So I know what it looks
like from the inside.

196
00:10:09,578 --> 00:10:12,428
I do not know what it looks
like across a closing table,

197
00:10:12,598 --> 00:10:16,648
and where this gets near deal mechanics
I am going to hand you to people who do.

198
00:10:17,518 --> 00:10:19,558
Here is the part that surprises people.

199
00:10:20,038 --> 00:10:24,608
In a large part of the country, you do not
need a licence to prepare a business for

200
00:10:24,658 --> 00:10:29,368
sale. Preparing a business and selling
one are treated as different jobs.

201
00:10:29,418 --> 00:10:32,268
For a long time this
practice has been possible.

202
00:10:32,438 --> 00:10:34,978
It has not been possible for one person.

203
00:10:35,028 --> 00:10:37,288
The reason is documentation.

204
00:10:37,338 --> 00:10:38,338
Interviewing an owner,

205
00:10:38,758 --> 00:10:43,868
writing up how the work actually happens,
assembling the records into something a

206
00:10:43,918 --> 00:10:45,388
stranger can follow.

207
00:10:45,878 --> 00:10:50,428
That used to be a team of people
and months of billable hours,

208
00:10:50,478 --> 00:10:54,228
which is exactly why the fee
never worked at this size.

209
00:10:54,278 --> 00:10:56,398
That is the part that changed.

210
00:10:56,448 --> 00:11:00,258
Drafting from a recorded conversation,
assembling a financial narrative,

211
00:11:00,458 --> 00:11:02,038
organising a data room.

212
00:11:02,088 --> 00:11:06,108
That is now the cheapest part of the
job instead of the most expensive.

213
00:11:06,158 --> 00:11:08,728
What has not changed is the other half.

214
00:11:08,778 --> 00:11:11,068
Deciding what a buyer
will actually discount.

215
00:11:11,268 --> 00:11:15,168
Working out what can be fixed inside
an engagement and what is structural.

216
00:11:15,638 --> 00:11:20,548
And telling a founder, out loud, that the
business depends on her in a way that is

217
00:11:20,598 --> 00:11:22,228
going to cost her money.

218
00:11:22,838 --> 00:11:24,008
No tool does that.

219
00:11:24,518 --> 00:11:25,848
That is the product.

220
00:11:26,398 --> 00:11:30,008
What do you sell first.

221
00:11:30,058 --> 00:11:31,158
One engagement.

222
00:11:31,208 --> 00:11:32,238
One business.

223
00:11:32,478 --> 00:11:34,248
Fixed scope, fixed fee.

224
00:11:34,818 --> 00:11:37,808
You take a baseline reading
against a published checklist.

225
00:11:38,038 --> 00:11:39,038
You interview.

226
00:11:39,158 --> 00:11:42,188
You write down how the work happens.

227
00:11:42,718 --> 00:11:46,948
You clean up the records so
a stranger can verify them.

228
00:11:46,998 --> 00:11:50,868
You look at who the
customers actually belong to.

229
00:11:50,918 --> 00:11:55,788
You find what only the owner can do,
and you move it or you document it.

230
00:11:56,208 --> 00:12:01,798
Then you assemble the package and you walk
her through what got fixed and what did

231
00:12:01,848 --> 00:12:02,768
not.

232
00:12:02,818 --> 00:12:06,568
And you are equally clear
about what you are not doing.

233
00:12:06,938 --> 00:12:09,408
You do not value the business.

234
00:12:09,658 --> 00:12:11,928
You do not find her a buyer.

235
00:12:12,178 --> 00:12:13,928
You do not negotiate.

236
00:12:14,198 --> 00:12:16,688
You do not advise on the transaction.

237
00:12:16,738 --> 00:12:20,168
Those are other people's jobs,
and some of them are licensed.

238
00:12:20,218 --> 00:12:22,968
Which brings us to the
constraint I promised you.

239
00:12:23,268 --> 00:12:26,328
The obvious way to price
this is a cut of the sale.

240
00:12:26,578 --> 00:12:28,368
Align yourself with the outcome.

241
00:12:28,658 --> 00:12:31,328
It is what everybody
else in that room does.

242
00:12:31,678 --> 00:12:32,508
You

243
00:12:32,558 --> 00:12:33,798
cannot do that.

244
00:12:34,198 --> 00:12:39,088
In a meaningful number of states, taking
a fee tied to the sale of a business is a

245
00:12:39,138 --> 00:12:40,818
regulated activity.

246
00:12:41,278 --> 00:12:44,728
Doing it without the right
licence is not a technicality.

247
00:12:45,198 --> 00:12:49,168
In some places it can cost you
every fee you earned on the deal.

248
00:12:49,818 --> 00:12:54,068
I am not going to name states, because
the line moves and I am not your lawyer.

249
00:12:54,258 --> 00:12:57,328
That is a real piece of
homework for wherever you are.

250
00:12:57,818 --> 00:13:00,148
But notice what that does to the business.

251
00:13:00,538 --> 00:13:02,848
It does not just change your invoice.

252
00:13:02,978 --> 00:13:05,488
It decides your entire model.

253
00:13:05,958 --> 00:13:08,388
Fixed fee is not a preference here.

254
00:13:08,578 --> 00:13:10,888
It is the shape the law leaves you.

255
00:13:11,458 --> 00:13:13,438
Think about a house changing hands.

256
00:13:13,488 --> 00:13:16,058
The agent gets paid when it completes.

257
00:13:16,108 --> 00:13:20,188
The surveyor gets paid for looking,
whether it completes or not.

258
00:13:20,238 --> 00:13:24,948
Those are different jobs with different
risk, and nobody finds that strange.

259
00:13:25,438 --> 00:13:26,828
You are the surveyor.

260
00:13:27,298 --> 00:13:30,038
Before you sell any of
that, answer one question.

261
00:13:30,238 --> 00:13:32,408
What are you actually promising.

262
00:13:32,458 --> 00:13:33,208
Not the sale.

263
00:13:33,258 --> 00:13:36,618
You do not control whether she
sells, or when, or for how much.

264
00:13:36,928 --> 00:13:39,608
Half the time she does
not control it either.

265
00:13:39,658 --> 00:13:42,688
So the thing you deliver is the checklist.

266
00:13:42,998 --> 00:13:47,038
Every item resolved, or deferred with
a reason, or ruled out of scope.

267
00:13:47,278 --> 00:13:48,688
And she signs it.

268
00:13:48,738 --> 00:13:50,428
That is the acceptance event.

269
00:13:50,478 --> 00:13:53,488
It is also the only version that stays on
the right side of the line we just talked

270
00:13:53,538 --> 00:13:57,548
about, because the moment your
payment depends on the deal,

271
00:13:57,598 --> 00:13:59,228
you are doing the regulated thing.

272
00:13:59,718 --> 00:14:03,558
Finding her is the next problem,
and the answer is that you do not.

273
00:14:03,718 --> 00:14:06,378
You find the people who
already know her instead.

274
00:14:06,428 --> 00:14:09,448
Accountants. Insurance
and wealth advisers.

275
00:14:09,498 --> 00:14:14,068
And brokers, who are the interesting one,
because a broker turns away businesses

276
00:14:14,118 --> 00:14:15,338
that are not ready,

277
00:14:15,778 --> 00:14:18,488
and every one of those is
a person who needs you.

278
00:14:18,638 --> 00:14:21,358
Your first conversation
is not with an owner.

279
00:14:21,538 --> 00:14:24,568
It is with a broker,
and it is one question.

280
00:14:24,618 --> 00:14:28,078
What do you do right now with
the ones you have to say no to.

281
00:14:28,128 --> 00:14:32,228
And I have to flag the risk in that,
because it is the thing most likely to sink

282
00:14:32,278 --> 00:14:36,768
this. It is possible brokers already do
a version of this for free to win the

283
00:14:36,818 --> 00:14:41,608
listing. If that is true everywhere, this
business does not have a front door.

284
00:14:42,058 --> 00:14:45,328
Finding out is the second thing you should
do, and I will tell you the first in a

285
00:14:45,378 --> 00:14:46,188
minute.

286
00:14:46,238 --> 00:14:50,288
Time to do the arithmetic out loud, because
this is where it gets uncomfortable.

287
00:14:51,158 --> 00:14:56,128
Everything from here is a modeled scenario,
not observed performance or an earnings

288
00:14:56,178 --> 00:15:00,848
forecast. These are my assumptions
and I will show you all of them.

289
00:15:01,798 --> 00:15:04,128
Say you charge twelve
thousand for an engagement.

290
00:15:04,378 --> 00:15:06,308
Say you deliver eight in a year.

291
00:15:06,538 --> 00:15:09,908
Eight times twelve thousand is
ninety six thousand of revenue.

292
00:15:10,518 --> 00:15:13,608
Take out your tools and your
overhead, call it ten thousand,

293
00:15:13,798 --> 00:15:19,608
and you are at eighty six thousand before
you pay yourself anything or pay any tax.

294
00:15:20,358 --> 00:15:22,308
Now put a number on what you need.

295
00:15:22,558 --> 00:15:24,448
Say you want a hundred
and twenty thousand.

296
00:15:24,718 --> 00:15:26,148
It does not clear.

297
00:15:26,398 --> 00:15:27,608
It is not close.

298
00:15:28,318 --> 00:15:32,148
To get to a hundred and twenty you need
about eleven engagements at that price.

299
00:15:32,478 --> 00:15:37,428
Eleven engagements at eighty five hours
each is nine hundred and thirty five hours

300
00:15:37,478 --> 00:15:40,948
of delivery, which is essentially
your entire working year,

301
00:15:40,998 --> 00:15:43,858
with nothing left for
finding the next client.

302
00:15:44,298 --> 00:15:46,508
So there are two honest ways out.

303
00:15:46,558 --> 00:15:49,768
You charge about sixteen
thousand instead of twelve.

304
00:15:50,238 --> 00:15:53,988
Or you accept less while you
work out whether this is real.

305
00:15:54,538 --> 00:15:58,088
And underneath all of that sits
one number nobody has measured,

306
00:15:58,138 --> 00:15:59,168
including me.

307
00:15:59,518 --> 00:16:02,388
How many hours does one
of these actually take.

308
00:16:02,678 --> 00:16:04,547
I have used eighty five.

309
00:16:04,718 --> 00:16:06,808
That is an assumption, not a finding.

310
00:16:07,438 --> 00:16:10,868
And the biggest piece of it is
time in a room with an owner,

311
00:16:10,958 --> 00:16:14,148
which is precisely the part
no tool makes shorter.

312
00:16:14,798 --> 00:16:16,948
That number decides this business.

313
00:16:17,178 --> 00:16:19,368
Not the fee. Not the market.

314
00:16:19,728 --> 00:16:20,728
That one.

315
00:16:20,958 --> 00:16:23,428
Something here is genuinely hard,

316
00:16:24,178 --> 00:16:25,618
and it is not the work.

317
00:16:26,038 --> 00:16:31,598
It is that you are asking somebody to pay
you today for something that pays off at

318
00:16:31,648 --> 00:16:34,008
an event with no date on it.

319
00:16:34,058 --> 00:16:37,828
Every business this resembles
has a deadline doing the selling.

320
00:16:37,878 --> 00:16:39,048
The audit is booked.

321
00:16:39,098 --> 00:16:40,988
The inspection is scheduled.

322
00:16:41,258 --> 00:16:42,508
You do not have that.

323
00:16:43,298 --> 00:16:46,728
What you have instead is the
thing we talked about earlier.

324
00:16:46,778 --> 00:16:49,178
About half of these exits are not planned.

325
00:16:49,598 --> 00:16:52,918
So the argument is not
get ready for your exit.

326
00:16:52,968 --> 00:16:57,928
The argument is that half the
time nobody gets a warning.

327
00:16:57,978 --> 00:17:00,048
That is a real argument.

328
00:17:00,098 --> 00:17:03,188
It is also a weaker one
than a date on a calendar,

329
00:17:03,238 --> 00:17:05,008
and I am not going to dress it up.

330
00:17:05,438 --> 00:17:07,148
So there are two things

331
00:17:07,518 --> 00:17:08,748
that would kill this.

332
00:17:09,318 --> 00:17:12,828
Owners who agree with
every word and still defer.

333
00:17:13,478 --> 00:17:17,268
And brokers who already do it for free.

334
00:17:17,318 --> 00:17:21,807
If either one is true where you
are, you should not build this.

335
00:17:22,318 --> 00:17:23,428
Go back to that table.

336
00:17:24,338 --> 00:17:26,808
He asks her what happens if
she is gone for a month.

337
00:17:26,978 --> 00:17:30,768
And this time she does not stop, because
the answer is not something she has to

338
00:17:30,818 --> 00:17:32,348
reconstruct on the spot.

339
00:17:32,438 --> 00:17:33,808
It is written down.

340
00:17:34,078 --> 00:17:34,928
He can read it.

341
00:17:34,978 --> 00:17:35,978
He can check it.

342
00:17:36,138 --> 00:17:37,908
Nothing about the business changed.

343
00:17:37,998 --> 00:17:40,308
It made money before
and it makes money now.

344
00:17:40,548 --> 00:17:43,828
The only difference is that
somebody made it inspectable.

345
00:17:44,258 --> 00:17:46,847
There it is. The work in the pause.

346
00:17:47,618 --> 00:17:52,368
And now you know what it honestly costs to
do, and roughly what it has to charge to

347
00:17:52,418 --> 00:17:53,418
be worth doing.

348
00:17:54,018 --> 00:17:55,948
My verdict is build.

349
00:17:56,798 --> 00:17:59,828
Not because this is
proven, because it is not.

350
00:18:00,258 --> 00:18:03,928
Nobody in my research had run this
practice and published what it earns.

351
00:18:04,188 --> 00:18:07,867
The base case in my own model does
not clear the number I set for it.

352
00:18:08,358 --> 00:18:13,118
Build means this is worth constructing
carefully enough to find out,

353
00:18:13,618 --> 00:18:16,208
and I have told you the two
things that would end it.

354
00:18:16,618 --> 00:18:19,648
Here is the first move,
and it costs you nothing.

355
00:18:21,598 --> 00:18:22,908
Write the checklist.

356
00:18:23,648 --> 00:18:29,408
Then find one owner you already know
and run the diagnostic for free.

357
00:18:29,458 --> 00:18:30,788
Not to be generous.

358
00:18:31,658 --> 00:18:33,208
To start a stopwatch.

359
00:18:33,998 --> 00:18:39,888
Because at the end of it you will know
the one number this entire business turns

360
00:18:39,938 --> 00:18:42,848
on, and right now nobody does.

361
00:18:46,798 --> 00:18:49,368
If you want more blueprints
taken apart like this one,

362
00:18:49,418 --> 00:18:52,048
including the parts that do
not survive the arithmetic,

363
00:18:52,278 --> 00:18:52,867
subscribe.
